          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong partial recursive function
            (often “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computable+function"}> computable function
            ”, but see there for disambiguation) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial function
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            which can be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definition"} defined
            by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithm"} algorithm
            or computer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/program"} program
            (e.g., a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Turing+machine"} Turing machine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            as inputs, and which on input may run forever, but otherwise eventually halts and returns a natural number as output.
          %p
            This idea as described is vague until it is circumscribed by a specific notion of computer program (Turing machines, register machines, abaci, etc.). There is a standard article of faith called the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Church-Turing+thesis"}> Church-Turing thesis
            ”, identifying functions on natural numbers that are algorithmically computable with those that are computable using a Turing machine (or some variant class of machines that is Turing-complete).
          %p A purely mathematical definition of the intended class of functions is given below.
          %h2#definition Definition
          #recursive.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em partial recursive function
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial function
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^k
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq 0
              is finite) that belongs to the smallest
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              of partial functions that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  includes all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+functions"} constant functions
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to \mathbb{N}
              %li
                %p
                  includes all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
                  maps
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi i
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mi k
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i: \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi …
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i = 1, \ldots, k
              %li
                %p
                  includes the successor function
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                          %mo :
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              %li
                %p
                  is closed under
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi k
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi k
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1: \mathbb{N}^{k} \to \mathbb{N}, \ldots, f_n: \mathbb{N}^{k} \to \mathbb{N}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: \mathbb{N}^n \to \mathbb{N}
                  belong to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi …
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mi k
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ (f_1, \ldots, f_n): \mathbb{N}^{k} \to \mathbb{N}
              %li
                %p
                  is closed under primitive
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursion
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi k
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi h
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 2
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: \mathbb{N}^{k+2} \to \mathbb{N}
                  belong to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N}^{k+1} \to \mathbb{N}
                  defined recursively by the equations (for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in \mathbb{N}
                  and
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{N}^k
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0, \mathbf{x}) = g(\mathbf{x})
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y+1, \mathbf{x}) = h(y, f(y, \mathbf{x}), \mathbf{x})
                %p
                  also belongs to
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %li
                %p
                  is closed under minimization: given any
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+function"} total
                  function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N}^{k+1} \to \mathbb{N}
                  belonging to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mi k
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\mathbf{x}) = c
                  iff
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(c, \mathbf{x}) = 0
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(d, \mathbf{x}) \gt 0
                  whenever
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi d
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi c
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq d \leq c-1
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em primitive recursive function
              is a function that belongs to the smallest class of functions of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}
              that contains constants, projection maps, the successor map, is closed under composition, and is closed under primitive recursion.
          %p
            Clearly the primitive recursive functions are a subclass of partial recursive functions. Notice that primitive recursive functions are not merely partial functions, but actual (
            %em> total
            ) functions.
          #Lawvere.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Both the class of partial recursive functions and the class of primitive recursive functions yield
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Th
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(Comp)
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Th
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi PrimRec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(PrimRec)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \to 1
              of the Lawvere theory is a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}^1
              belonging to the class. It is straightforward to check that in either case, the generating object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              of the Lawvere theory (or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              if you prefer) is a parametrized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} NNO
              in that category: this is the essence of primitive recursion.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em recursive relation
              (or what is more usual nowadays, a
              = succeed ")" do
                %em computable relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^k
              whose characteristic function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi: \mathbb{N}^k \to \{0, 1\}
              is recursive. Similarly, a primitive recursive relation is a relation whose characteristic function is primitive recursive.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p We may build up a stock of functions and relations that are primitive recursive as follows. (All closure properties mentioned will clearly also apply to partial recursive functions and relations.)
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Addition, multiplication, and exponentiation on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \mapsto n!
                is primitive recursive. Binomial coefficients.
            %li
              %p
                The predecessor function
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Pred
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pred: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Pred
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pred(0) = 0
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Pred
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pred(n+1) = n
            %li
              %p
                %em> Truncated subtraction
                , where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mover
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{\cdot}{-} y = x - y
                if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \geq y
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{\cdot}{-} 0 = x
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi Pred
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{\cdot}{-} (y+1) = Pred(x \stackrel{\cdot}{-} y)
            %li
              %p
                The distance function
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi x
                          %mo −
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|x-y|} = (x \stackrel{\cdot}{-} y) + (y \stackrel{\cdot}{-} x)
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(x) = 1 \stackrel{\cdot}{-} x
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(\alpha(x))
            %li
              %p
                Therefore the equality relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|x-y|} = 0
                is a primitive recursive relation. So is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mover
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \stackrel{\cdot}{-} x
                doesn’t equal zero”. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is a (primitive) recursive function, then its graph defined by the relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = f(x)
                is a (primitive) recursive relation.
            %li
              %p
                (Boolean combinations) Similarly, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is a primitive recursive relation (so that its characteristic function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R
                is primitive recursive), then so is its negation
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg R
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mrow
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi R
                        %mo =
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{\neg R} = \alpha(\chi_R)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                are primitive recursive relations, so is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo ∧
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \wedge Q
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mrow
                            %mi P
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi Q
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi P
                        %mo ⋅
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{P \wedge Q} = \chi_P \cdot \chi_Q
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x, y, \mathbf{z})
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(y)
                are primitive recursive, so is
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(y, \mathbf{z}) \coloneqq \sum_{x=0}^{h(y)} f(x, y, \mathbf{z})
              %p
                and similarly with the sum replaced by a product. It follows that primitive recursive predicates are closed under
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em bounded quantification
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x, y, \mathbf{z})
                is a primitive recursive predicate and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
                is a primitive recursive function, then the predicate
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                defined by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi x
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi y
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(y, \mathbf{z}) = \forall_{0 \leq x \lt y} R(x, y, \mathbf{z}) \coloneqq \bigwedge_{x=0}^{y-1} R(x, y, \mathbf{z})
              %p
                is primitive recursive since
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi Q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msubsup
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo =
                            %mn 0
                          %mrow
                            %mi y
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_Q(y, \mathbf{z}) = \prod_{x=0}^{y-1} \chi_R(x, y, \mathbf{z})
            %li
              %p
                (Bounded least choice operator) If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x, y, \mathbf{z})
                is a primitive recursive relation and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
                is a primitive recursive function, then the function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y, \mathbf{z})
                defined to be “the least
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq h(y)
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x, y, \mathbf{z})
                if such
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                exists, else
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi w
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⋅
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y, \mathbf{z}) = [\sum_{x=0}^{h(y)} (x \cdot \chi_R(x, y, \mathbf{z}) \cdot (\prod_{w=0}^{x-1} \chi_{\neg R}(w, y, \mathbf{z}))] + y \cdot \prod_{x=0}^{h(y)} \chi_{\neg R}(x, y, \mathbf{z}).
            %li
              %p
                (Pairing and unpairing) There is an isomorphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^2 \to \mathbb{N}
                in the Lawvere theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Th
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi PrimRec
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(PrimRec)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N}^2 \to \mathbb{N}
                with a primitive recursive inverse
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                to be the function
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mi m
                            %mo +
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                        %mo )
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: (m, n) \mapsto \binom{m+n+1}{2} + n
              %p
                Its inverse
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                takes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
                to the pair
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mover
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo −
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mover
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mo ⋅
                            %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                        %mo )
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi m
                      %mo −
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mover
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mo ⋅
                            %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                        %mo )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \stackrel{\cdot}{-} (m - \binom{a \stackrel{\cdot}{-} 1}{2}+2), m - \binom{a \stackrel{\cdot}{-} 1}{2})
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is the least element less than
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m+2
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                            %mi a
                            %mn 2
                          %mo )
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \binom{a}{2} \gt m
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                is manifestly primitive recursive.
          %p
            As a consequence of this last property, there exist primitive recursive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^k
            for any
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \gt 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            itself. (The exception is the case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to \mathbb{N}
            is recursive.)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              To show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^2 \cong \mathbb{N}
              in the category of primitive recursive functions, it is not enough just to exhibit a primitive recursive bijection
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N}^2 \to \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Th
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi PrimRec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(PrimRec) \to Set
              (see Remark
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Lawvere"}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#inverse"}
              below.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p Similarly, it is not always true that if a graph of a function is a primitive recursive relation, then the function itself is primitive recursive. (For example, the graph of the Ackermann function, discussed below, is a primitive recursive relation.) However, we do have a sample theorem as follows.
          #bound.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If a graph of a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a primitive recursive relation, and if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \leq g
              for some primitive recursive
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is itself primitive recursive.
          %p
            The proof can be roughly expressed as follows: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x, y)
            is the functional computable relation, then let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            be the least
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq g(x)
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x, y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            is primitive recursive.
          %p The point hovering in the background is that there are some computable functions which grow faster (in fact, much much much faster) than any primitive recursive function. This underscores the important role of the minimization axiom for partial recursive functions, which allows unboundedly large searches to take place. Indeed, we have the following crucial fact:
          #graph.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi N
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq{N}^2
              is a graph of a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and is a recursive relation, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a (total) recursive function. (The converse holds by one of the properties listed above.)
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              According to the minimization axiom in Definition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#recursive"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R: \mathbb{N}^2 \to \{0, 1\}
              is a recursive function, the function that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to the least
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in \mathbb{N}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 - \chi_R(x, y) = 0
              is also recursive. But this function is simply
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              The inverse of a recursive bijection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is also recursive.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              The graph of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a recursive relation, and so is the opposite graph, which is the graph of the function
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
          %p Before passing on to general recursive functions, it is good to have some idea of the scope of primitive recursive functions. Some examples:
          %ul
            %li
              The function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(n) = p_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi th
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
              prime, is primitive recursive.
          %h2#the_ackermann_function The Ackermann function
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              For each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              by
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 2
                        %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0(m) = 2 m
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi n
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{n+1}(m) = (A_n)^m(1)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^m
              denotes the composition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              copies of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %strong Ackermann function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              is defined by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(m) = A_m(m)
          %p
            (The function is named after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilhelm+Ackermann"} Wilhelm Ackermann
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi m
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0(m)=m+1
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{n+1}(m)=(A_n)^m(f(1))
          %p
            We show that while each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
            is primitive recursive, the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            grows faster than any primitive recursive function on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p
            By property 1 above,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0
            is primitive recursive. Supposing that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
            is primitive recursive,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = A_{n+1}
            is also primitive recursive because it satisfies the recursion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(0) = 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(m+1) = A_n (\phi(m))
          %p
            By induction one may easily show
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n(1) = 2
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n(2) = 4
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{n+1}(3) = A_n(A_n(A_n(1))) = A_n(A_n(2)) = A_n(4)
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1
            gives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
            powers of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_2
            gives tetrations (iterated exponentials stacked
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            high) of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_3
            gives iterated tetrations, and so on.
          %p Some routine inductions establish the following facts:
          %ul
            %li
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
              is strictly increasing, and with the sole exception of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0(0) = 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \lt A_n(m)
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
              is strictly inflationary in arguments
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \gt 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \lt A_n(3)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}
              is primitive recursive, then there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi max
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_1, \ldots, x_k) \leq A_n(\max \{3, x_1, \ldots, x_k\})
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \ldots, x_k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is
              %strong dominated
              by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              In the case where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is constant with value
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=m
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              the successor, use
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i: \mathbb{N}^k \to \mathbb{N}
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi max
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i = \pi_i(x_1, \ldots, x_k) \leq A_0(\max \{3, x_1, \ldots, x_k\}).
            %p
              Now proceed by induction on the construction of primitive recursive functions. Given that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N}^k
              is dominated by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi m
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1, \ldots, g_k: \mathbb{N}^m \to \mathbb{N}
              are dominated by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
